.TH CHTTPCLIENT_SET_MAX_RESPONSE_BODY_SIZE 3 "2026-07-30" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpclient_set_max_response_body_size \- cap the size of a response body in the buffer
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpclient.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpclient_set_max_response_body_size(chttpcli cli,
                                                      size_t max_bytes);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function caps the size of a response body in the buffer. The cap
works in all three tiers: it covers chttpclient_do,
chttpclient_do_async, the pooled-sync wrappers on top of
chttpclient_do_async such as chttpclient_do_pooled, chttp_get,
chttp_post, chttp_put, chttp_delete, chttp_patch and chttp_run_query.
Give 0, which is the default, to go back to buffering with no limit.

The cap has no effect on chttpclient_do_streaming,
chttpclient_do_async_streaming or chttpclient_do_pooled_streaming. A
caller that streams already controls its own memory with the value
that chttpcli_write_fn gives back (a value less than len aborts the
transfer), so this cap has nothing to bound on that path.

The client applies the cap in two ways. A response whose
Content-Length header alone declares more than max_bytes fails at
once, before the client reads one byte of the body off the wire. A
chunked body, or a body that ends with the close of the connection,
declares no length up front, so the client checks such a body as it
arrives and fails the request at the moment the total body so far goes
past max_bytes. Both cases give ccol_msg_too_large: chttpclient_do
gives it, the rv of the async result holds it, and
chttpclient_do_pooled gives it. The client does not reuse that
connection afterwards, and treats it like the connection of any other
malformed response.

This cap does not apply to a redirect hop, whatever the declared size
and the real size of the body of such a hop: the client always drops
the body of an intermediate hop and never buffers it. Only the body of
the final response that the caller gets counts against max_bytes.

The check of the declared length up front applies only to a message
that will really deliver the body to the caller. The declared
Content-Length of a 1xx informational response is exempt: RFC 7230
3.3.2 says that a server that obeys the standard never sends one on a
1xx response, but a server that misbehaves, or an attacker, can send
one, and the client always drops such a response and never gives it to
the caller. The declared Content-Length of a response to HEAD is exempt
too, because it describes what a GET gives back (RFC 7231 4.3.2), and
no real body bytes come after it.

You can call this function before or after the first request. It takes
effect at once for every request that follows, and does not change a
request that is already in flight.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cli       The client handle. It must not be CHTTPCLI_INVALID.
max_bytes The maximum size in bytes of a response body in the buffer.
          0 means no limit.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success      The client has the new cap.
ccol_invalid_args cli is CHTTPCLI_INVALID, or a stale handle of a
                  client that is already destroyed.
.fi
